msgid "Export the narrative"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Make a file of the story to send, print or publish, or copy the link of "
"its live reader.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The story is exported from its own window, with the **Export ▾** button "
"in the header of the **Narrative** window. Every file is a snapshot of "
"the story at the moment of export: the blocks that show the graph become "
"figures, citations or query cells, and what no person has validated stays"
" out unless you choose otherwise."
msgstr ""

msgid "Before you start"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Open the story in the Narrative workspace. When the graph holds several "
"stories, pick the one to export with its name at the top of the page."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Look at **Print** in the header of the window: it shows what Word and "
"LaTeX will contain, with a gap marked **does not go out: to be verified**"
" for each AI paragraph not validated yet. **Notebook** shows the cells of"
" the Jupyter file."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The files are made by the s3Dgraphy library through EMStudio's local "
"service. When the service does not answer, EMStudio says so and nothing "
"is written."
msgstr ""

msgid "Export a file"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **Export ▾** in the header of the **Narrative** window. The menu "
"lists:"
msgstr ""

msgid "**HTML · snapshot**"
msgstr ""

msgid "One page, frozen at the moment of export, to send by e-mail."
msgstr ""

msgid "**DOCX**"
msgstr ""

msgid "A Word document with the figures baked in and the sources cited."
msgstr ""

msgid "**LaTeX + .bib**"
msgstr ""

msgid "A LaTeX document with its bibliography, ready to compile without EMStudio."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Jupyter**"
msgstr ""

msgid "A notebook whose cells ask the graph instead of quoting it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Under the list, a line says how many AI paragraphs not validated, and how"
" many nodes made with AI that nobody verified, will stay out of the "
"files; when there are none it says **All the text is validated by a "
"person**. Installed plugins can add further formats to this menu."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Choose a format. If something is not validated, a window opens first, "
"with the title saying how many paragraphs stay out. It lists them chapter"
" by chapter: a click on **paragraph** and its number takes you to it in "
"**Write**, where you can verify it (see :doc:`ai-drafts`). Then choose:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Export without them**, followed by how many stay out, the proposed "
"choice: the file leaves them out;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Export anyway, with ⚠︎ beside them**: the file keeps them, marked, for "
"this export only;"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Cancel**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The file is downloaded with the name of the graph, and a message confirms"
" the format."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The LaTeX export arrives as a ``.zip`` when the story has figures: it "
"holds ``main.tex``, ``main.bib`` and a ``fig/`` folder, because LaTeX "
"keeps its figures as separate files. A story without figures arrives as a"
" ``.tex`` file with its ``.bib`` beside it, in two downloads."
msgstr ""

msgid "What the blocks become"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **Inspector** of each block says, under **Outside EMStudio**, how it "
"will come out. In Word and LaTeX:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"a **Matrix** becomes a figure, a slice of the same Matrix as on the "
"canvas;"
msgstr ""

msgid "a **Table** becomes a figure with the rows that its filters keep;"
msgstr ""

msgid "a **Timeline** becomes a figure of the epochs on their axis;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"a **Map** becomes a figure of its vector layer (the marker, the footprint"
" when it is known, the north arrow, the coordinates), without the "
"background tiles;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"a **Document** becomes its picture under its citation, with its entry in "
"the bibliography;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"a **3D scene** becomes the picture of the framing saved in the block (see"
" :doc:`embeds`); without a saved framing it stays a placeholder with its "
"link;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"a standard block, such as the **Bibliography**, is written as the text of"
" its answer, with the date it was computed;"
msgstr ""

msgid "a mention keeps the name of its node in the sentence."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the Jupyter notebook the blocks become cells that query the graph, and"
" a 3D scene stays a link. A figure that cannot be drawn leaves its "
"placeholder, and the export still happens."
msgstr ""

msgid "Copy the link of the live reader"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Live reader · link** copies the address of the reader: the page where "
"the story is read with its blocks resolved at every opening and its 3D "
"navigable, for people who do not use EMStudio. The reader reads a "
"published study, so the link is made for a study opened from the Catalog "
"of a node. For a study opened from the disk, EMStudio answers **The live "
"reader reads a published study: open this one from the Catalog (or "
"publish it) to get its link**."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A document to carry away, in the format the reader needs: an HTML page "
"for an e-mail, a Word file for a colleague, a LaTeX project for a "
"publication, a notebook that asks the study again each time it runs. The "
"story itself stays in the graph, where you keep writing it."
msgstr ""

msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`/reference/export-formats` lists every file EMStudio writes."
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`ai-drafts` for validating the AI paragraphs before exporting."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`/how-to/files/pin-a-version` to keep the version of the study that "
"a document cites."
msgstr ""

